            <ArticleTitle>ST-elevation acute myocardial infarction due to arterial thrombosis in a 29-year-old woman with normal coronary arteries.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>59-61</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Acute myocardial infarction (AMI) is rare in young adults. We present a case of a 29-year-old black woman who presented with an acute onset of chest pain while sleeping. Anterior wall ST-elevation AMI was diagnosed based on clinical presentation, electrocardiographic findings, and elevated cardiac biomarkers. Coronary angiography revealed a totally occluded proximal left anterior descending artery. The obstructing lesion, thrombus, was removed. There was no evidence of atherosclerotic disease or dissection. An evaluation for a hypercoagulable state was unrevealing. Echocardiography 1 year later revealed normal left ventricular wall motion and systolic function.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
